course: medicine
lesson: 43
title: Running, gymnastics and martial arts
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Your muscles have a fast fuel for sprints that runs low within about a minute, which is why the kid who blasts off in the 800 metres is walking by lap two. Long races are won with an even pace and a sprint saved for the end.
explain: Gymnastics is about control: a forward roll lands on the back of the shoulders with the chin tucked, never on the top of the head, and flips are learned over months with a coach, spotters and thick mats, never from a video on the sofa.
explain: Stretching works on warm muscles, slowly, to a gentle pull; sharp pain means stop. And a trampoline is one person at a time, because bodies bouncing together crash into each other.
explain: Martial arts start with control and respect: judo's first lesson is how to fall safely, and karate teaches you to avoid fights and use your skills only to get away from danger.
fact: Running flat out uses the muscles' fast fuel, which runs low within about a minute, so in longer races an even pace works better.
fact: Junior parkrun is a free 2 km run on Sunday mornings for ages 4 to 14, and walking and jogging are welcome; the adult parkrun on Saturday mornings is 5 km.
fact: Soreness a day or two after a race comes from tiny muscle strains being repaired; lactic acid is cleared within about an hour.
fact: Usain Bolt ran 100 m in 9.58 seconds in 2009, still the world record; sprinters reach top speed around halfway and the winner is often the one who slows least.
fact: In exercise a very fast heartbeat is normal for a healthy child; chest pain or fainting is not and must be told to an adult.
fact: In a forward roll the chin tucks to the chest so the back of the shoulders takes the weight; rolling on the top of the head bends the neck badly.
fact: Muscles stretch further and more safely when warm; forcing a stretch into sharp pain can tear a muscle, and flexibility grows slowly over weeks.
fact: RoSPA advises one person on a trampoline at a time, with safety netting and pads; most trampoline injuries happen when more than one is on, as lighter children get flung and crash into others.
fact: Flips can land you on your head or neck if anything goes wrong; gymnasts learn them only with a coach, spotters and thick mats.
fact: Martial arts train balance, fast reactions and control; children's sparring stops before contact, and the first rule is to avoid fights; a club can throw out a pupil who uses it to fight outside.
fact: Judo has been in the Olympics since 1964 and taekwondo since 2000.
fact: Judo starts with break-falls: chin tucked and the arm slapping the mat spread the landing and keep the head off the floor; sticking a straight arm out can break a wrist.
exercise: Tyler sprints flat out at the start of the 800 m and walks by lap two. Why? | His muscles' fast fuel ran low within about a minute | His lungs filled up with lactic acid, which is very heavy | His shoes slowed him on the bend | The second lap is longer than the first one
exercise: How many laps of a 400 m track is 800 m? | 2 | 4 | 8 | 1
exercise: What's the best plan for an 800 m race? | Start steady and save a sprint for the end | Sprint flat out from the gun | Walk the first lap, then sprint the whole second one | Stop halfway for a drink and a breather
exercise: Freya runs a junior parkrun in Cardiff. How long is it? | 2 km | 5 km | 10 km | 42 km
exercise: How much longer is an adult 5 km parkrun than a junior 2 km one? | 3 km | 7 km | 2 km | 10 km
exercise: Arthur can barely walk downstairs two days after cross-country. What causes it? | Tiny muscle strains being repaired | Lactic acid stuck in his legs for days | Tiny broken bones in both of his feet | Running out of blood in the legs
exercise: What is Usain Bolt's 100 m world record? | 9.58 seconds | 5.8 seconds | 15.9 seconds | 19.58 seconds
exercise: In a 100 m race, where do sprinters reach top speed? | Around halfway | In the very first step | Right at the finish line, as they dip | They never reach a top speed at all
exercise: Hannah counts 45 beats in 15 seconds after running. Beats a minute? | 180 | 60 | 90 | 45
exercise: Which sign during running must be told to an adult? | Chest pain or fainting | A fast, pounding heartbeat | Fast, heavy breathing | Sweating and a red face
exercise: Lily-Rose rolls on the top of her head. How should a forward roll go? | Chin tucked, weight on the back of the shoulders | Head down flat, roll right over on the top of the head | Arms straight, land on the hands and push off | Back arched, land softly on the neck
exercise: Ava tries the splits cold. Her sister says push harder if it hurts. What's right? | Warm up first and stretch to a gentle pull | Push into sharp pain to get bendy | Bounce into it as hard as she can | Stretch only when muscles are cold
exercise: Four cousins pile onto a trampoline together. What does RoSPA advise? | One person at a time | Four is fine if there's a safety net round it | As many as fit on it without touching | Two at a time, holding hands for balance
exercise: Mason plans to learn a backflip from TikTok on the sofa. What's the rule? | Flips only at a gym club with a coach | Fine on a soft sofa or a bed with cushions | Fine if he watches the video slowly twice | Fine with a friend filming and spotting
exercise: Rohan gets his karate belt: "I'm gonna batter Josh now." What do clubs teach? | Avoid fights; skills are for safety | Use skills to win playground fights quickly | Show off moves on smaller kids | Fight anyone who annoys you
exercise: Niamh spends her first judo lesson falling. What is the break-fall for? | Spreading the landing and keeping the head off the floor | Making the throws hurt the other person more | Wasting time before the real judo starts | Landing on a straight arm
exercise: How many years apart did judo and taekwondo join the Olympics? | 36 | 26 | 64 | 20
test: Tyler: "My legs have just quit, mate." What would happen if he'd run an even pace? | He'd likely finish faster and stronger | He'd be even slower and more tired | His legs would quit even sooner | Nothing would change at all, pace doesn't matter
test: Freya: "Lush (lovely) morning for it!" Her mate says you have to be fast for parkrun. True? | No, walking and jogging are welcome | Yes, slow runners get sent home after a lap | Yes, there's a time limit of 8 minutes for juniors | No, but only adults may run
test: Arthur: "It's lactic acid, innit." What's the better explanation? | Muscle repair; lactic acid was gone in about an hour | Lactic acid stays in the legs for a week | His leg bones are slowly cracking | He ran out of water in his muscles and they dried up
test: Dev: "I could beat Usain Bolt, no cap, he's old now." A fast 10-year-old runs 100 m in about 16 s. About how far behind the record time? | About 6.4 seconds | About 0.6 seconds | About 25.6 seconds | About 1 second
test: Hannah: "My heart's going mental, is that bad?" After running round the field. What's the truth? | Normal, unless there's chest pain or fainting | Dangerous, she must lie down at once | Her heart is going to break from the strain | She must never run fast again
test: Lily-Rose: "Ow, my neck went crunch." What would have kept her neck safe? | Chin tucked, rolling on her shoulders, teacher watching | Rolling faster on the top of her head | Doing it on a hard floor for grip | Keeping her head up and back the whole time
test: Big sis: "If it hurts, push harder, that's how you get bendy." What happens if Ava does? | She could tear a muscle; flexibility grows over weeks | She'd be doing the full splits by tonight | Pain makes the muscles stretch further and safely | Nothing, stretches can't hurt
test: Thabo: "Lekker, we're all jumping together!" Four on the trampoline. What usually happens? | The lighter ones get flung and crash into others | Everyone bounces higher and safer together | Nothing, the nets round the side stop every single injury | The trampoline just bounces less
test: "No cap, I'll learn a backflip off TikTok tonight." Why is it a bad plan? | A mistake can land him on his head or neck | Backflips are banned everywhere, even in gyms | He'll get too good, too fast | Sofas are too bouncy to be any fun
test: Rohan wants to batter Josh. What would happen at most clubs if he fought outside? | He could be thrown out of the club | He'd get his next belt sooner for being brave | His sensei would cheer him on from the side | Nothing, it's karate
test: Niamh: "Wee bit boring, so it is." Why learn falling first? | So throws are safe for everyone | Because judo is mostly just falling over | To make the lesson last longer and tire them | To learn to land on a straight arm
test: In an action film, a hero lands from a roof on one straight arm. What would likely happen in real life? | A broken wrist or worse | A perfect landing every single time | Nothing, arms are made to catch falls like that | A bounce back up to the roof
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a runner who commences an 800-metre race at maximum velocity subsequently decelerates markedly. | The rapidly available muscular fuel is depleted within approximately one minute | Lactic acid accumulates in the lungs and obstructs respiration | The second lap exceeds the first in length | The footwear loses its grip upon the track
exam: Select the response consistent with the established technique for a forward roll. | The chin is tucked and the weight is borne upon the shoulders | The weight is borne upon the crown of the head | The arms are locked straight upon landing | The back is arched throughout
exam: Pursuant to the advice of RoSPA, determine the number of persons who should occupy a domestic trampoline simultaneously. | One | Two at most | Four, if light | As many as the netting permits
exam: Ascertain the principal rule taught to children upon commencing instruction in a martial art. | That conflict is to be avoided and skills used only for safety | That opponents are to be overcome outside the club as well as within it | That belts confer the right to fight | That contact in sparring is unrestricted
